  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why men tend to have larger Adam's apples:\n\n### What Is the Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (the voice box). It helps protect the walls and the frontal part of the larynx, including the vocal cords, which are located directly behind it.\n\n### Do Only Men Have Them?\n\nWomen do have Adam's apples, but they are usually much less prominent than in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable.\n\n### Why Are Men's Larger?\n\nThe main reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n1. **Equal size at birth:** The Adam's apple is about the same size in people assigned male at birth and those assigned female at birth until puberty.\n\n2. **Testosterone drives growth:** Once puberty starts, hormones (especially testosterone) in males may cause their vocal cords to grow bigger. The thyroid cartilage may therefore stick out a bit more to protect these larger vocal cords.\n\n3. **Larger voice box = deeper voice:** Because grown men have larger voice boxes, their Adam's apples are a lot more prominent. This is also the reason why men speak in deeper tones.\n\n4. **Difference in shape:** There is also a difference in the shape of the laryngeal prominence between males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females, it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n5. **Position matters too:** In females, the laryngeal protrusion sits on the upper edge of the thyroid cartilage, and the larynx tends to be smaller in size, so the \"bump\" is much less visible or not discernible.\n\n### Can Women Have a Prominent Adam's Apple?\n\nYes! Women who have higher levels of testosterone, either naturally or due to medical conditions, may experience more masculine traits, including a larger larynx and a more noticeable Adam's apple. Conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) can cause an increase in testosterone levels in women, potentially contributing to a more prominent laryngeal prominence.\n\n### Is It a Medical Concern?\n\nAn Adam's apple isn't a medical concern and won't cause any health problems. Having (or not having) one depends on your own unique genetic makeup.\n\nIn summary, the larger Adam's apple in men is primarily driven by the surge of testosterone during puberty, which causes the larynx and surrounding cartilage to grow significantly more than in women.",
